[36m(TaskRunner pid=24255)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=24255)[0m [response] To determine the interest earned on a deposit of $10,000 at 4.5% compounded annually over two years, we use the formula for compound interest:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=24255)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=24255)[0m \[ A = P \left(1 + \frac{r}{n}\right)^{nt} \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=24255)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=24255)[0m where:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=24255)[0m - \( A \) is the amount of money accumulated after n years, including interest.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=24255)[0m - \( P \) is the principal amount (the initial amount of money).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=24255)[0m - \( r \) is the annual interest rate (decimal).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=24255)[0m - \( n \) is the number of times that interest is compounded per year.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=24255)[0m - \( t \) is the time the money is invested for in years.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=24255)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=24255)[0m In this problem, \( P = 10000 \), \( r = 0.045 \), \( n = 1 \), and \( t = 2 \). Plugging these values into the formula, we get:
